### Pattern notes
- Interviewers want versioning, immutability and loud failures on bad inputs.
- Patterns: Registry/Repository; immutable value objects; **Strategy** if you later support Jinja vs format syntax. Fail fast at registration and render time.
- Follow-up answers: A/B via deterministic bucketing by user-id hash; roll back with a movable "production" label pointing at a version, not deletion.
- Learn from: [awesome-low-level-design](https://github.com/ashishps1/awesome-low-level-design), [Strategy](https://refactoring.guru/design-patterns/strategy)

### Key insight
Treat templates like code releases: append-only, immutable versions, validated at registration, and every render stamped with the version. Validation is set arithmetic on the placeholder names.

### Approach
1. `register`: parse with `string.Formatter().parse` (raises on malformed input), reject non-identifier fields, skip if text equals the latest, else append a new frozen `PromptTemplate` with version `len + 1`.
2. `get`: latest or `history[version - 1]`, with `KeyError` otherwise.
3. `render`: `missing = expected - given`, `extra = given - expected`; raise `PromptInputError` if either is non-empty; otherwise `format(**variables)` and return a `RenderedPrompt` with the version.

### Complexity
Register and render are O(template length); get is O(1).

### Common mistakes
- Regex placeholder parsing that breaks on `{{literal}}` JSON examples.
- Silently ignoring extra variables (usually a typo in the caller).
- Mutating a template in place, so outputs logged against "v2" no longer match v2.
- Global version counters instead of per-name.

### Talking points
Rollback: keep a `labels` map (`production -> 3`) and move the pointer. A/B: `hash(user_id) % 100 < split`. Traceability: log `(name, version, rendered hash, model, output id)`.

